<Base
  title="Research — Waking Matter"
  description="Questions we are willing to work on: how useful learning can be preserved, inherited, and compounded across agents, models, and time."
  canonical="https://wakingmatter.com/research/"
  current="research"
>
  <main id="content" class="wrap">
    <header class="page-hero">
      <p class="label">Research</p>
      <h1>Questions, not findings.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        This page describes an area of inquiry. It is not a paper list, a lab
        report, or a claim that the work is done. Neighboring fields already
        name parts of it: continual and lifelong learning, distillation and
        adapters, retrieval and external memory, replay, model merging. We do
        not claim those fields. For the long form, read
        <a href="/notes/intelligence-should-accumulate/">Intelligence should accumulate</a>.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose-page">
      <p class="label">Current questions</p>
      <div class="inquiry">
        <article>
          <p class="meta">01 — Preserve</p>
          <h2>What can survive a change of mind?</h2>
          <p>
            Storage is cheap. A usable representation is not. How can learning
            remain inspectable and revisable when the model, the tools, or the
            people around it are replaced?
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="meta">02 — Inherit</p>
          <h2>How does later work begin from earlier work?</h2>
          <p>
            Inheritance that cannot be examined is a rumor. What interfaces
            would make prior distinctions the default starting point for a new
            agent, a new model, or a new person, without pretending the past
            is always right?
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="meta">03 — Compound</p>
          <h2>When is keeping it actually cheaper?</h2>
          <p>
            An archive is not a tradition. Under what conditions does later
            work get clearer, cheaper, or more ambitious because earlier work
            was kept — and how would we notice if it did not?
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="meta">Open</p>
          <h2>What should remain local?</h2>
          <p>
            Privacy, a failed path that should die, a distinction that is only
            true in one room. Locality is not the same problem as revision. We
            leave the boundary open.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
